  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 207 AM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nOver the weekend, the upper-level pattern continues to be \nsouthwesterly as the trough slowly moves to the northeast from \nthe Four Corners region and into the southern Rockies. Toward \nthe surface, low- to mid-level moisture is being fed into the \narea from the south as return flow from a high pressure system \nto the east. This will likely lead to a cold front moving into \nthe area midday Saturday and allow for prolonged showers Sunday.\nWith the cold front moving in, weak and low-topped \nthunderstorms are possible as CAPE is still the main driving \nforce with values being above 300 J/kg. If showers does occur, \nit is likely favored to the east and over the K-25 corridor on \nSaturday and south of I-70 on Sunday. While confidence for \nshowers on both days remain high (75%), there is low confidence \n(20-25%) for thunderstorms to occur before turning into showers \non Sunday. Temperatures are expected to drop back into seasonal \nranges being in the 60s and 50s. \n\nStarting next week, the trough is forecast to move north of the\narea midday Monday. For the remainder of the period, the \npattern becomes unclear as guidance spread starts to show two \npossible scenarios of what could happen. The first scenario \ncould be that zonal flow starts to form over the area keeping \ntemperatures in the 50s. The secondary scenario is a secondary \ntrough to the north that could push a cold front into the area \nand potentially drop temperatures further and into the 40s. \nHowever,with the unclear change in the pattern, overnight \nshowers from Sunday and into Monday are still slightly possible \nas the trough moves through and keeping temperatures for each \nday being in the 50s.",
